  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nStorm Chances Today/Tonight: A strong 40-45kt LLJ has developed over \nover the central and northern Plains early this morning spurring on \nscattered storms across central MN into far western WI. As the LLJ \nshifts east early this morning storms will push into central and \nnorth-central WI. There is enough (250-500 J/kg) elevated \ninstability for these storms to continue producing lightning as well \nas sporadic strong wind gusts and hail through the morning. Storm \nactivity will gradually decrease late morning and afternoon as the \nLLJ weakens, however, CAMs do show some isolated storms percolating \nthrough much of the day as return flow brings a narrow ribbon of \nmoist and weakly unstable air into the region. Another uptick in \nstorm activity is expected this evening/overnight as a cold front \nswings across the region. There is a marginal (1/5) risk for strong \nto severe storms along and just ahead of the front despite rather \nunimpressive thermal profiles as a seasonally strong 40-50kt 500mb \njet overspreads the region. One aspect to monitor with any storms \nwill be high rainfall rates as PWATs are expected to near 2\" and \nwarm cloud depths of 10-12kft will promote efficient warm rain \nprocesses. Storm motions will not be overly slow, so any flood \nthreat may mainly be tied to any training storms.\n\nAlong with the storm chances today expect breezy south winds with a \ntightening pressure gradient. There is a 30-50% chance for peak wind \ngusts over 30 mph across northern WI while areas in central and east-\ncentral WI may see wind gusts peak around 20-25 mph this afternoon.\n\nRest of the Forecast: A few isolated showers may linger through \nearly Wednesday morning as the aforementioned front pushes east of \nthe region. Dry conditions are then forecast for the remainder of \nWednesday through much of Friday. Following the frontal passage , a \ntight pressure gradient will develop on Wednesday leading to gusty \nwesterly winds of 30-35 mph. The next chance of showers and storms \narrives Friday night into Saturday with a cold frontal passage. \nConfidence is low on timing and placement of convection. Aside from \nnear normal temperatures on Thursday, but temperatures will remain \nabove normal.",
